    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Nissan Seeks Adventures at 2026 SEMA Show in Las Vegas With Frontier, Pathfinder, Armada Concepts\nVideo: SkyDrive eVTOL Completes Demonstration Flight for Japan’s State Officials\n9 photos\nPhoto: SkyDrive\nSkyDrive, the AAM (Advanced Air Mobility) company that has developed what is now the most acclaimed, domestically and globally, Japanese eVTOL (electric vertical takeoff and landing), was proud to receive an official visit from Japan’s Finance Minister, signaling an even closer focus on this new-generation aircraft and a new era of mobility.\nAt the end of October, a yellow eVTOL with bright red accents took to the sky and hovered over a flight test field in Yamaguchi City, Yamaguchi Prefecture. It was a SkyDrive aircraft, and the flight was a demonstration performed specifically for Japan’s Finance Minister, Satsuki Katayama.\nIt was a short flight of less than 10 minutes, during which the uncrewed eVTOL reached a top altitude of 30 meters (98 feet) and covered a little over 2 km (1.2 miles), flying at 78 kph (48 mph). That was enough to showcase the SD-05’s core capabilities, and Minister Katayama was particularly impressed with how quiet the electric aircraft was, and how quickly it was able to change direction.\nThe flight demonstration was followed by further discussions about the aircraft’s design and technology, and Minister Katayama also took a closer look at the interior of another SD-05, the one that had performed previous demonstration flights in Tokyo and at Expo 2025 Osaka.\nOne of the most important parts of this official visit was that representatives from SkyDrive’s official manufacturing partner were also present for talks with the Minister. That partner happens to be Suzuki Motor Corporation, which makes SkyDrive one of the very few eVTOL makers worldwide that are backed by big automotive names (the most notorious being the partnership between Archer Aviation and Stellantis for the Midnight eVTOL).\nIn 2024, SkyDrive announced it would start official aircraft production at a facility owned by Suzuki, and a year later, Suzuki brokered a major deal for SkyDrive in India, one of its biggest international markets, for a pre-order of up to 50 eVTOLs, from the Gujarat-based JetSetGo Aviation Services.\nDuring this important visit, Suzuki representatives also discussed manufacturing plans with Minister Katayama, and SkyDrive representatives were confident that this event marked a step forward for their ambitious commercialization plans. Earlier this year, the Japanese Government issued a “Growth Strategy” officially confirming eVTOLs as one of the key technologies considered priorities for major investments.\nSkyDrive is still in the process of securing Type Certification from the Japan Civil Aviation Bureau (JCAB), as well as eyeing future FAA (Federal Aviation Administration) certifications that will allow it to commercialize the SD-05 in the US.\nThis past summer, SkyDrive and FAA representatives met in Washington D.C., in the context of the 2026 FAA-EASA International Aviation Safety Conference, in preparation for kicking off the FAA certification process officially for the Japanese autonomous eVTOL.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\njapanese evtol SkyDrive Suzuki evtol air taxi\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT YouTuber Shuts Down SEMA With 960 Cu-In 13-Rotor Quad-Turbo Miata, It's Seven Feet Tall",
